{"causes":["The Second Vatican Council (1962-1965) called for a renewed catechetical approach in the Catholic Church.","The need for a comprehensive synthesis of Catholic doctrine emerged from a desire to educate the faithful in the post-Vatican II context.","Pre-existing catechetical materials and Church traditions provided a foundation for the creation of a formal catechism."],"effects":["The CCC has become the authoritative text for Catholic doctrine, influencing catechesis around the world.","It has facilitated ecumenical dialogue by providing a clear presentation of Catholic beliefs to other Christian traditions.","The publication of the CCC has led to a resurgence in interest in systematic theology and moral teachings within the Church."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"pope-john-paul-ii","sourceName":"Pope John Paul II","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church","targetName":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","context":"Pope John Paul II initiated the compilation and publication of the Catechism in 1992, emphasizing its importance for the Church."},{"sourceSlug":"second-vatican-council","sourceName":"Second Vatican Council","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church","targetName":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","context":"The Second Vatican Council set the stage for the development of the Catechism by calling for a renewed approach to catechesis."},{"sourceSlug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church","sourceName":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"theology-of-the-body","targetName":"Theology of the Body","context":"The CCC incorporates teachings on human sexuality and ethics, notably the Theology of the Body, illustrating its comprehensive scope."},{"sourceSlug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church","sourceName":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"catholic-doctrine","targetName":"Catholic Doctrine","context":"The CCC serves as the definitive reference for Catholic doctrine, shaping the understanding of faith for millions."},{"sourceSlug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church","sourceName":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"catholic-education","targetName":"Catholic Education","context":"The publication of the CCC significantly influenced the curriculum in Catholic education systems worldwide."}],"places":[{"name":"Vatican City, Vatican","role":"Publication site"},{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Major theological center"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Cultural influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Catechism of the Catholic Church","type":"Reference article","slug":"catechism-of-the-catholic-church"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q75561","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catechism_of_the_Catholic_Church"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Catechism of the Catholic Church contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Catechism of the Catholic Church (Latin: Catechismus Catholicae Ecclesiae) is the official compendium of Catholic doctrine and teaching, promulgated by Pope John Paul II on 11 October 1992 — the thirtieth anniversary of the opening of the Second Vatican Council — and published in its definitive edition (the editio typica) in Latin in 1997. It is the first universal catechism since the Roman Catechism (Catechismus Romanus) promulgated after the Council of Trent in 1566 — four centuries previously — and reflects the teaching of Vatican II while presenting the whole of Catholic doctrine in a systematic, accessible form intended for the universal Church. Comprising 2,865 numbered paragraphs organized in four 'pillars' (The Creed; The Sacraments; The Commandments; and Prayer), the Catechism covers the entirety of Catholic belief and moral teaching, from the doctrine of the Trinity and Christology through the seven sacraments, through the moral theology of the commandments (including the Church's positions on abortion, contraception, capital punishment, war, social justice, and sexuality), to a systematic treatment of prayer culminating in the Our Father.

The Catechism was initiated at the Extraordinary Synod of Bishops (1985) called to evaluate the reception of Vatican II twenty years after its conclusion, and produced by a commission of twelve cardinals and bishops chaired by Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger (later Pope Benedict XVI) over six years of drafting (1986–1992). Its promulgation under John Paul II — who wrote a special Apostolic Constitution (Fidei Depositum) for its presentation — was a major moment in the pontificate, consolidating the interpretation of Vatican II in a way that both welcomed the Council's renewal and resisted more radical post-conciliar interpretations.

The Catechism has been translated into more than forty languages and has sold tens of millions of copies worldwide — the best-selling catechism in the history of the Catholic Church. Its systematic presentation of Catholic teaching on every aspect of belief, sacramental life, ethics, and prayer makes it the primary reference point for Catholic doctrine for the world's 1.3 billion Catholics and for those seeking to understand Catholic teaching.","subjects":["Catholic Church","Contemporary Era","Theology","Christianity","Religious Texts","Doctrine","20th Century","Vatican"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Catechism of the Catholic Church has shaped contemporary Catholic education and practice, influencing millions and fostering ecumenical dialogue with other Christian denominations.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
